  </figure>

  <h2>2. Make cancelling a sale someone else's decision</h2>
  <p>
    The easiest way to take cash is to take payment, then cancel or refund the sale once the customer has gone. If any
    cashier can do that on their own, the record shows nothing and the cash leaves with them.
  </p>
  <p>
    The fix is simple: a paid sale can only be voided or refunded by a supervisor or manager, and every void stays on
    the record with the cashier's name. Most cashiers will never need it. The ones tempted to use it will know it is
    seen.
  </p>

  <div class="guide-zt">
    <p class="guide-zt__h">How ZapTill does it</p>
    <p>
      Voiding a paid order or giving a refund needs a manager's PIN, and every void shows up against the cashier's name
      in the day's report. Discounts and sales on account work the same way: they are recorded, by name.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>3. Treat M-Pesa like cash</h2>
  <p>
    M-Pesa is safer than cash, but it has its own tricks: a payment marked as M-Pesa that never arrived, a fake
    confirmation message, or a sale paid half in cash and recorded as all M-Pesa. The habits that help:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>The cashier checks the confirmation on the business phone or the M-Pesa statement, not the customer's screen.</li>
    <li>Split payments are recorded as split: so much M-Pesa, so much cash.</li>
    <li>At the close, the M-Pesa total for each cashier is compared with what actually came in.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    ZapTill prints your Buy Goods or Paybill number on every receipt, records split payments, and gives you the M-Pesa
    total for each cashier at the day close. Comparing that with your Safaricom statement is still your job: ZapTill does
    not read the statement for you.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Stock is cash on a shelf</h2>
  <p>
    A crate of soda, a bottle of whisky, a tray of meat or a jerrycan of engine oil is money. If it leaves without a
    sale, it is the same as cash leaving the drawer, only harder to notice.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Check deliveries in.</strong> Count what arrives against the supplier's note before you sign.</li>
    <li><strong>Count the expensive things often.</strong> You do not need a full stock-take every week. Count the ten items that cost you most, every week, and the rest once a month.</li>
    <li><strong>Record wastage.</strong> Spoiled milk, a dropped bottle, expired yoghurt: write it off on the day, with a reason, so it does not hide inside "missing".</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    In ZapTill, deliveries, counts, wastage and transfers between branches are all recorded against the stock, so the
    gap between what should be on the shelf and what is there shows up, item by item.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Look at the day close every day</h2>
  <p>
    Every check above is useless if nobody looks. The single most effective habit is boring: read the day close every
    night, even when you are tired, even when you are away. Staff notice very quickly when the owner reads it. They also
    notice when the owner stops.
  </p>

  <figure>
    <Laptop label="The day close on the ZapTill web dashboard">
      <DayCloseScreen />
    </Laptop>
    <figcaption>The day close on the web dashboard: each cashier's M-Pesa, cash expected and counted, over and short. Demo data.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    With the ZapTill Cloud, the day close is on your phone and a daily summary arrives by email, so you can read it
    wherever you are. With ZapTill Analytics, the Watch list also warns you about patterns worth a look: many voids or refunds by one
    cashier, unusual discounts, drawers that keep coming up short.
  </p>

  <div class="guide-note">
    <p>
      <strong>A warning is not proof.</strong> A cashier with many voids may simply work the busiest shift. Use the
      numbers to ask a calm question, not to accuse. Most of the time the answer is training, a faulty process or a
      tired evening. When it is not, the record, by name and by day, is what lets you act fairly.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>A short checklist</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Each cashier has their own PIN and their own drawer or shift.</li>
    <li>The float is counted in and the drawer counted out, every shift.</li>
    <li>Only a manager can void a paid sale or give a refund.</li>
    <li>M-Pesa is checked on your phone or statement, and split payments are recorded as split.</li>
    <li>Deliveries are counted in; the expensive items are counted every week; wastage is written off on the day.</li>
    <li>You read the day close every night.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    None of this needs a point of sale. A cash book and discipline can do most of it. A point of sale makes it
    automatic, puts a name on every number, and saves you from adding it all up by hand at midnight. If you want to put
    a rough figure on what small leaks could be costing you, try the <a href="/pricing/#calc-title">leak calculator</a>.
  </p>
</Guide>
